{"name":"Jealousy","hex":"#7fab60","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jealousy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of being jealous; a jealous attitude.","A close concern for someone or something, solicitude, vigilance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jealousy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jealousy","blocks":[{"text":"Jealousy generally refers to the thoughts or feelings of insecurity, fear, and concern over a relative lack of possessions or safety."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/%C4%90%C3%A1nh_ghen.JPG/330px-%C4%90%C3%A1nh_ghen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jealousy","wikidata":"Q6545811"},{"title":"Jealousy (X Japan album)","blocks":[{"text":"Jealousy is the third studio album by Japanese heavy metal band X Japan, then known as simply X. The album was released on July 1, 1991, by Sony, as the band's second major-label release. Jealousy is the band's best-selling album, having sold more than one million copies. It topped the Oricon chart and stayed on the chart for 50 weeks. The album's singles would also reach the top three on the chart. It is their last album under the name X, before changing to X Japan, and the last to feature Taiji on bass, who would be replaced by Heath. In 2018, readers and professional musicians voted Jealousy the sixth best album in the history of hard rock and heavy metal in We Rock magazine's \"Metal General Election\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d4/Xjapan_Jealousy.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jealousy_(X_Japan_album)","wikidata":"Q585102"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_Moholy-Nagy%2C_gelosia%2C_1927%2C_fotomontaggio_%28george_eastman_museum%2C_rochester_NY%29.jpg/500px-L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_Mo